<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/ice-storm.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/ice-storm-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/ice-storm.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/">Services</a></li><li aria-current="page">Ice Storm &amp; Freeze Appraisal</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Ice, Freeze and Burst-Pipe Claims Across North Mississippi</h1><p class="lede">Mississippi winters are usually mild, which is exactly why a hard freeze or ice storm does so much damage. Homes here aren’t built for it, and pipes, gutters and trees pay the price.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="hero-facts"><li><small>Region</small><b>North Mississippi, statewide freezes</b></li><li><small>Damage</small><b>Pipes, gutters, limbs, roofs</b></li><li><small>Events</small><b>1994, 2021, 2022</b></li></ul></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><h2>Winters Mississippi remembers</h2>
<p>The February 1994 ice storm coated North Mississippi in heavy ice, bringing down trees and power lines across a wide area and leaving many homes without power for days or weeks. In February 2021, a winter storm froze the entire state, bursting pipes in homes and businesses that rarely see temperatures that low. The December 2022 hard freeze brought another wave of frozen and broken plumbing.</p>
<p>Each event created a spike in claims at once, and carriers working through a backlog don’t always look closely. That’s when estimates come in thin.</p>
<p>Long outages bring their own losses. When power stayed off for days, food spoiled, sump pumps stopped and some homes went cold enough for pipes to freeze. Food spoilage and power-outage coverage vary by policy, and some forms pay only when the outage starts on the insured premises. Those are coverage questions, but once they’re accepted, the amounts still need support.</p>
<h2>Gutters, limbs and the roof edge</h2>
<p>Ice is heavy. When it builds on trees and gutters, the damage is mechanical:</p>
<ul>
<li>Gutters pulled away from fascia, bent hangers and cracked downspouts.</li>
<li>Fascia and soffit damage where gutters tore loose.</li>
<li>Limbs punching through shingles and decking, or scraping roof surfaces.</li>
<li>Broken skylights, satellite mounts and vent caps.</li>
<li>Fences, carports and outbuildings flattened by falling limbs.</li>
<li>Interior leaks where ice dams formed on low-slope sections or valleys.</li>
</ul>
<p>Carriers sometimes pay for a few feet of gutter rather than full runs, or overlook the fascia behind them. Large limbs bring the same removal sublimits as whole trees on many policies; see <a href="/claim-advice/ice-storm-tree-claims-north-mississippi/">ice storm tree claims in North Mississippi</a> for details.</p>
<h2>Frozen pipes and the water that follows</h2>
<p>When pipes freeze and split, the leak often isn’t found until the thaw. Water runs inside walls and under floors, sometimes for hours. The resulting claim looks like any other plumbing loss: tear-out, drying, flooring, cabinets, drywall and contents.</p>
<p>Freeze breaks often surface in places nobody checks right away: exterior walls behind kitchen sinks, crawlspace lines under older homes, ice-maker and washer supply lines, and attic plumbing in two-story houses. Garage water heaters and outdoor hose bibs crack as well. We trace the water from the break point outward, so the estimate covers every area that got wet, not just the room where the leak showed up.</p>
<p>Many policies exclude freeze damage if the home was vacant or unheated and reasonable care wasn’t taken to maintain heat or drain the system. That’s a coverage question. If the carrier has accepted the loss and the dispute is about the amount, appraisal can help. Our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/water-plumbing-loss-appraisal/">water and plumbing loss appraisal</a> page covers the line items in more depth.</p>
<div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Commercial sprinklers too</b><p>Frozen fire-sprinkler lines in churches, warehouses and offices can release a lot of water fast. Those losses often bring business income questions alongside the building repair, and appraisal can value both when coverage isn’t in dispute.</p></div>
<h2>Where freeze claims cluster</h2>
<p>North Mississippi sees the most ice: Oxford, Tupelo, Southaven, Olive Branch, Corinth, Starkville and Columbus. Older homes with crawlspaces and uninsulated plumbing are especially vulnerable, as are student rentals left empty over winter break in Oxford and Starkville. But the 2021 storm showed that freezes reach the Pine Belt, Metro Jackson and the Coast as well.</p>
<h3>Before you sign off on the claim</h3>
<p>Check whether the carrier’s estimate includes full gutter runs, fascia repair, all damaged roof slopes, interior water damage beyond the obvious, and drying documentation. If the numbers look light, we can review them and, if the gap is meaningful, serve as your appraiser. See our <a href="/counties-and-cities/oxford-lafayette-county/">Oxford and Lafayette County</a> and <a href="/counties-and-cities/tupelo-lee-county/">Tupelo and Lee County</a> pages for local detail.</p>
<h2>Available across Mississippi</h2>
<p>Ice Storm &amp; Freeze Appraisal is available statewide. Inspections are scheduled around your timeline in places such as <a href="/counties-and-cities/vicksburg-warren-county/">Vicksburg</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/columbus-lowndes-county/">Columbus</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/greenville-washington-county/">Greenville</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/jackson-hinds-county/">Jackson</a>.</p>
<h2>Ice Storm &amp; Freeze Appraisal questions</h2><div class="faqs"><details><summary>Is freeze damage covered if I was away from home?</summary><p>Many policies exclude freeze losses when a home was left unheated without draining the pipes. Read your policy’s freezing provision and ask a Mississippi attorney if the carrier raises it.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal value damage from the February 2021 winter storm now?</summary><p>Possibly, but deadlines matter. Your policy and Mississippi law set limits on when you can act, so confirm your timeline with a Mississippi attorney.</p></details><details><summary>Are torn gutters from ice covered as storm damage?</summary><p>Often yes, since weight of ice is a common covered peril. The dispute is usually about how many feet of gutter and how much fascia need replacing.</p></details></div>
